<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0; font-size: 24px;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 16px;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 10th Grade (CEFR: B1.1)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Visiting Our Country</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Interesting Things to See</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Create instructions for a task in personal life. Interactive: Compile feedback for collaborative writing in a structured manner.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can write instructions for visiting a local attraction (e.g., "To reach the museum, walk two blocks from the bus station"). Can compile and organize peer suggestions into a cohesive guide for must-see locations, adding personal insights and recommendations (e.g., "Several classmates recommended Casco Viejo for its historical significance, and I agree because its restored buildings offer a glimpse into Panama's past."). Can provide a written response to a friend's question about the best places to visit (e.g., "In the past, I visited the old town, and it was an unforgettable experience. I know that today, there are more restaurants and walking areas, making it even more enjoyable for visitors.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 20px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f0f8ff;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: left; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: left; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: left; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher projects images of famous Panamanian attractions (e.g., Panama Canal, Casco Viejo, Biomuseo, San Blas Islands). Students are asked to briefly write down 2-3 adjectives or short phrases describing each attraction in their notebooks. Then, they quickly write one "must-see" location they would recommend and one reason for their choice, activating vocabulary related to "Interesting Things to See."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher displays a short written example of instructions for visiting a local landmark or a recommendation for a specific activity. For instance: "To reach the Biomuseo, first go straight for two blocks on Amador Causeway. Then, turn left at the traffic light. The museum will be on your right, opposite the ferry terminal." The teacher explicitly models how to write clear, concise instructions using imperative verbs and prepositions of direction (go straight, turn left, across from, opposite). The teacher also models the pronunciation of key vocabulary like "interesting" (/ˈɪn.trə.stɪŋ/) and "restaurant" (/ˈres.trɒnt/), focusing on reduced syllables, and contrastive sounds such as /st/ in "stay" and /ʃ/ in "showcase" when introducing or reviewing relevant terms.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When we write instructions, should they be long and detailed, or short and to the point?" (Short and to the point)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do we use strong personal opinions or clear, factual directions when guiding someone?" (Clear, factual directions)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Visiting Our Country")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> An excerpt from a local Panamanian travel blog or an online city guide focusing on a specific attraction (e.g., "A Day in Casco Viejo: Your Guide to Panama's Historic Quarter"). The text includes clear instructions on how to reach the location, descriptions of "must-see" spots, historical context, and recommendations for activities. It naturally incorporates past simple/present perfect for historical facts and changes (e.g., "Casco Viejo was once a neglected area, but after its restoration, it has become a popular destination"), future tenses for suggested plans (e.g., "You will explore the charming streets, and we're going to have lunch at a local restaurant"), and examples of reported speech (e.g., "Our guide mentioned that the best seafood is found in the nearby market").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Writing: Productive: Create instructions for a task in personal life.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students read the travel blog excerpt and underline all verbs indicating direction (e.g., *go straight, turn left, navigate*) and prepositions of location (e.g., *across from, next to, between*).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students identify and write down 3 key instructions from the text (e.g., how to reach the area) and 2 "must-see" recommendations. For each, they identify the grammatical tense used (e.g., Past Simple for historical events, Present Perfect for recent developments, Future for plans).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Writing)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Practice using past simple and present perfect for contrasting actions over time, future with "will" and "going to" for intentions, reported speech, and vocabulary for directions (go straight, turn right, across from) and attractions (museum, market, beach, landmark, breathtaking, unique).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Grammar Transformation (Controlled Practice):</b> The teacher provides a list of sentences, and students rewrite them to practice target grammar.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Rewrite sentences using past simple and present perfect: "The old church stood alone. Now, it's a restored cathedral." (Students write: "The old church once stood alone, but it has since become a beautifully restored cathedral.")</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Convert direct speech to reported speech: "The tour guide said, 'You should visit the fish market for fresh seafood.'" (Students write: "The tour guide said that we should visit the fish market for fresh seafood.")</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Complete sentences with "will" or "going to": "We ______ (plan) a trip to the beach next week. I _______ (definitely book) the accommodations soon."</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Direction Writing (Semi-controlled Practice):</b> Students receive a simple, unlabeled map of a small, fictional town showing key landmarks (a museum, a market, a restaurant, a park, a cathedral). The teacher designates a starting point and an endpoint. Students write 4-5 clear sentences giving directions from the start to the end, using vocabulary like *go straight, turn right, turn left, across from, next to, between*, and verbs like *navigate, direct*.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Collaborative Recommendation Draft (Peer Check):</b> In pairs, students select a local attraction (e.g., a specific park, a historical area, a beach). They draft a short paragraph (3-4 sentences) recommending this place to a friend, including why it's a "must-see" (e.g., "The area showcases our cultural heritage") and one interesting historical fact or a planned future activity there. They must incorporate at least one instance of past simple/present perfect and one future tense structure. After drafting, pairs exchange their paragraphs with another pair and provide written feedback on clarity, grammar, and vocabulary usage.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Visiting Our Country")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Written Travel Guide Entry for a Local Attraction.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b> Students work in small groups (3-4 members). Each group chooses a specific Panamanian "Interesting Thing to See" (e.g., the Panama Canal Visitor Center, a specific beach in San Blas, Cerro Ancón). Their task is to collaboratively write a comprehensive travel guide entry for this attraction, targeting a friend who is visiting the country. The entry must include:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">A compelling description of the attraction using at least three appropriate adjectives (e.g., *breathtaking, tranquil, unique, wonderful*).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clear, step-by-step instructions on how to get there from a common reference point (e.g., "From the Albrook Bus Terminal, go straight for three blocks and then turn right...") using a minimum of four prepositions/verbs of direction (e.g., *go straight, turn right, across from, next to*).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Relevant historical context or recent developments using both past simple and present perfect tenses (e.g., "The landmark *was* inaugurated in 1914, and it *has since become* a symbol of national pride.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Recommendations for specific activities or things to see/do at the attraction, incorporating future tenses ("will" and "going to") to express plans (e.g., "I *will book* a boat tour for us," "We *are going to explore* the interactive exhibits").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">At least one piece of advice or recommendation presented using reported speech (e.g., "My friend said that the best time to visit is early in the morning").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The entry should utilize at least 7-10 vocabulary words from the recommended list (e.g., *visit, area, location, attraction, plan, direction, market, museum, restaurant, beach, town, history, guide, itinerary*).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Peer Assessment using a checklist and Teacher Observation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Groups exchange their completed written travel guide entries from Stage 4 with another group. Each group uses a provided checklist to assess the other's work, providing constructive written feedback on specific areas.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Peer Checklist & Teacher Observation):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity and accuracy of instructions for reaching the attraction (directions are easy to follow).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate and varied use of target vocabulary (nouns, verbs, adjectives, prepositions).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct application of past simple and present perfect for contrasting actions over time.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct application of "will" and "going to" to express future intentions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accurate use of reported speech for recommendations.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Overall coherence, organization, and grammatical accuracy of the guide entry.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Task completion: Does the guide fulfill all required sections as per the instructions in Stage 4?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Individually, students respond in writing to the following prompts:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was the most challenging aspect of collaboratively writing the instructions and recommendations for our travel guide?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Identify one specific writing tip or grammatical structure you learned or reinforced today that will help you write better travel advice in the future."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How effectively did our group incorporate all the required grammar structures (past simple/present perfect, future tenses, reported speech) and key vocabulary into our travel guide? What could we have improved?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What specific written feedback did you receive from your peers that helped you improve your writing, and how will you apply that learning in your next writing task?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> "Did students successfully apply the target grammar and vocabulary for writing clear instructions and engaging recommendations? Which specific grammar point (e.g., distinction between past simple and present perfect, correct use of reported speech) needs more explicit practice or scaffolding in future lessons? Was the collaborative writing process effective, and how can I further support groups in organizing and compiling feedback? What adjustments are needed for clarity in instructions or to challenge students in developing more complex sentences in their writing?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> "In our next and final lesson (Lesson 5), we will integrate all our learning about 'Visiting Our Country' by individually creating a comprehensive multimedia presentation of a travel itinerary for a visitor. This project will allow you to consolidate the descriptive language, clear instructions, and engaging recommendations we've practiced today, applying your 21st-century skills to create a compelling final product."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>